## Change Package: OTLP Ingress Sender-Allow (Ports 4317/4318)

**Scope:** Modify the sender-allow rule on the OTLP gRPC (4317) and OTLP HTTP (4318) server blocks in `otlp-ingress.conf` so that `10.99.0.0/27` (the `exporterCidrBlock` consumed verbatim from FABRIC) is the sole permitted sender, replacing the current `allow all;` on both ports. `operator-access.conf` (port 14318), the collector's own config, and all Prometheus/Grafana surfaces are untouched, per Program Interface Contract `observability.ingressScope` and the Phase 1 design's per-target change list. No device/service mutation occurs in this task — apply is out-of-band and human-gated.

## Baseline Evidence (restated verbatim from Phase 0 Harvest / Phase 1 Design)

**`otlp-ingress.conf` — current sender-allow: NONE (`allow all;`, no `deny`, both blocks)**, source `get_ingress_config` (as-deployed file mount):

```nginx
server {
    listen 4317;
    http2 on;

    location / {
        allow all;

        grpc_pass grpc://172.31.114.10:4317;
    }
}

server {
    listen 4318;
    client_max_body_size 16m;

    location / {
        allow all;

        proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;
    }
}
```

**`operator-access.conf` (14318) — already scoped to `172.20.0.1`, annotated by harvest as rig plumbing, not the sender-allow surface. No overlap with `10.99.0.0/27` (different /16). Untouched by this package.**

**Collector config (`get_otel_config`)** — OTLP receiver bound to `172.31.114.10:4317`/`:4318` on a private network reachable only via `obs-ingress`; no source-IP enforcement capability there; `otlp-ingress.conf` is the confirmed sole enforcement point. No change authored.

**Prometheus / Grafana** — harvest-confirmed neither surface has a job/board on 4317/4318; no change authored.

## Desired-State Configuration Files

### File 1 — `otlp-ingress.conf` (MODIFIED — full file, both server blocks edited together, single revision)

```nginx
# OTLP ingress allowlist — the lab's enforcement point for "which senders may export telemetry".
# Fronts BOTH OTLP receivers: gRPC (4317) and HTTP (4318). The collector accepts OTLP only on a
# private network shared with this proxy, so these two server blocks are the only way in.
# nginx evaluates allow/deny IN ORDER, first match wins.
#
# Sender-allow updated per Program Interface Contract `observability.senderAllowRule`:
# the exporter CIDR block consumed from FABRIC (10.99.0.0/27) becomes the sole permitted
# sender on both declared OTLP ports. The prior unrestricted `allow all;` is replaced, not
# kept, on both blocks. No other listener on this host is modified.

# OTLP/gRPC
server {
    listen 4317;
    http2 on;

    location / {
        allow 10.99.0.0/27;
        deny all;

        grpc_pass grpc://172.31.114.10:4317;
    }
}

# OTLP/http
server {
    listen 4318;
    client_max_body_size 16m;

    location / {
        allow 10.99.0.0/27;
        deny all;

        proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;
    }
}
```

### File 2 — `operator-access.conf` — **NOT MODIFIED**

Out of scope per contract `ingressScope` ("to the exclusion of any other listener on the host") and per Phase 1 name-collision check (no overlap with `10.99.0.0/27`). No file emitted; the operator makes no edit here.

### Collector config (`get_otel_config`) — **NOT MODIFIED**

No source-IP enforcement capability exists at this layer; no file emitted.

## Consumed Values

```json
[{"kind": "cidr", "value": "10.99.0.0/27"}]
```

Consumed verbatim from the FABRIC leg's published crossing value and the Phase 1 design's own `## Consumed Values` block — not recomputed, not widened.

## Chosen-Value Rationale

This package introduces **no new numeric thresholds, timeouts, or intervals** — the only substantive value in the diff is the sender CIDR itself, which is a **consumed** value (fixed by contract, not authored here) and must not be recomputed. The one design choice within this leg's discretion is the **directive form and ordering** used to enforce it:

| Alternative considered | Evaluated against harvested state | Chosen? |
|---|---|---|
| `allow 10.99.0.0/27; deny all;` (allow-then-deny, first-match-wins) | nginx evaluates `allow`/`deny` in the order they appear per the harvest's own header comment ("nginx evaluates allow/deny IN ORDER, first match wins"); this ordering is the only form that yields "CIDR is sole permitted sender" — omitting the trailing `deny all;` would leave the block open (default nginx `ngx_http_access_module` behavior with no matching rule is **allow**), silently reintroducing `allow all;`'s effect | **Yes** |
| `deny all; allow 10.99.0.0/27;` (deny-first) | Under first-match-wins, `deny all;` at the top would match every request unconditionally and never reach the `allow` line below it — this would block the exporter range too, a functional regression, not a tightening | No — would break intended senders |
